  ],
  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Pronouns and 'to be'",
      "body": "**Identity** ('X is Y') needs no verb in everyday Odia: ମୋ ନାମ ସାମ୍ 'my name [is] Sam', ସେ ଡାକ୍ତର 'he/she [is a] doctor'. (Formal writing adds **ଅଟେ** *ôṭe*.) The negative is **ନୁହେଁ** *nuheṃ* 'is not'.\n\n**Location or state** ('X is in Y', 'X is well') uses **ଅଛି** *ôchhi*, which agrees with the subject:\nମୁଁ ଅଛି *muṃ ôchhi* — I am\nଆମେ ଅଛୁ *ame ôchhu* — we are\nତୁ ଅଛୁ *tu ôchhu* — you are (intimate)\nତୁମେ ଅଛ *tume ôchhô* — you are (familiar)\nଆପଣ ଅଛନ୍ତି *apôṇô ôchhônti* — you are (formal)\nସେ ଅଛି *se ôchhi* — he/she is\nସେ ଅଛନ୍ତି *se ôchhônti* — he/she is (respectful)\nସେମାନେ ଅଛନ୍ତି *semane ôchhônti* — they are\n\nThe negative of ଅଛି is **ନାହିଁ** for every person: ସେ ଘରେ ନାହିଁ 'he/she isn't at home'. Notice there is **no gender** anywhere: ସେ is both 'he' and 'she'.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "ମୁଁ ଜଣେ ଛାତ୍ର।", "roman": "muṃ jôṇe chhatrô.", "en": "I am a student." },
        { "native": "ସେ ଡାକ୍ତର ନୁହେଁ।", "roman": "se ḍaktôrô nuheṃ.", "en": "He/She is not a doctor." },
        { "native": "ଆମେ ଭୁବନେଶ୍ୱରରେ ଅଛୁ।", "roman": "ame Bhubôneswôrôre ôchhu.", "en": "We are in Bhubaneswar." },
        { "native": "ସେ ଏବେ ଘରେ ନାହାନ୍ତି।", "roman": "se ebe ghôre nahanti.", "en": "He/She (respected) is not at home now." },
        { "native": "ଏହା ମୋର।", "roman": "eha morô.", "en": "This is mine." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Question words stay in place",
      "body": "To ask a question in Odia, put the question word **where the answer would be** — no change of word order and no 'do/does'. Compare: ମୁଁ **ଭୁବନେଶ୍ୱରରେ** ରହେ 'I live in Bhubaneswar' → ଆପଣ **କେଉଁଠି** ରହନ୍ତି? 'Where do you live?'\n\nThe main question words: **କ'ଣ** what · **କିଏ** who · **କେଉଁଠି** where · **କେଉଁଠୁ** from where · **କେମିତି** how · **କାହିଁକି** why · **କେବେ** when · **କେତେ** how much/many · **କେଉଁ** which.\n\nYes/no questions just use rising intonation, optionally with **କି** at the end.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "ଏ କିଏ?", "roman": "e kie?", "en": "Who is this?" },
        { "native": "ଆପଣଙ୍କ ଦେଶ କେଉଁଠି?", "roman": "apôṇôṅkô desô keuṃṭhi?", "en": "Where is your country? / Which country are you from?" },
        { "native": "ତୁମେ ଓଡ଼ିଆ କାହିଁକି ଶିଖୁଛ?", "roman": "tume Oṛia kahiṃki sikhuchhô?", "en": "Why are you learning Odia?" },
        { "native": "ଏହା କ'ଣ?", "roman": "eha kôṇô?", "en": "What is this?" },
        { "native": "ଆପଣ ହିନ୍ଦୀ ଜାଣନ୍ତି କି?", "roman": "apôṇô Hindi jaṇônti ki?", "en": "Do you know Hindi?" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "dialogue": {
    "title": "At the tea stall",
    "setting": "Sam, a British visitor, gets chatting with Bapi at a roadside tea stall in Bhubaneswar.",
    "lines": [
      { "speaker": "Bapi", "native": "ନମସ୍କାର! ଆପଣଙ୍କ ନାମ କ'ଣ?", "roman": "nômôskarô! apôṇôṅkô namô kôṇô?", "en": "Hello! What's your name?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "ନମସ୍କାର। ମୋ ନାମ ସାମ୍। ଆପଣଙ୍କ ନାମ?", "roman": "nômôskarô. mo namô Sam. apôṇôṅkô namô?", "en": "Hello. My name is Sam. Your name?" },
      { "speaker": "Bapi", "native": "ମୋ ନାମ ବାପି। ଆପଣ କେଉଁଠୁ ଆସିଛନ୍ତି?", "roman": "mo namô Bapi. apôṇô keuṃṭhu asichhônti?", "en": "My name is Bapi. Where are you from?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "ମୁଁ ଲଣ୍ଡନରୁ ଆସିଛି। ଏବେ ମୁଁ ଭୁବନେଶ୍ୱରରେ ରହେ।", "roman": "muṃ Lôṇḍônôru asichhi. ebe muṃ Bhubôneswôrôre rôhe.", "en": "I'm from London. Now I live in Bhubaneswar." },
      { "speaker": "Bapi", "native": "ବାଃ! ଆପଣ ଓଡ଼ିଆ କହିପାରନ୍ତି?", "roman": "baḥ! apôṇô Oṛia kôhiparônti?", "en": "Wow! Can you speak Odia?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "ଟିକିଏ। ମୁଁ ଓଡ଼ିଆ ଶିଖୁଛି।", "roman": "ṭikie. muṃ Oṛia sikhuchhi.", "en": "A little. I'm learning Odia." },
      { "speaker": "Bapi", "native": "ବହୁତ ଭଲ! କାହିଁକି ଶିଖୁଛନ୍ତି?", "roman": "bôhutô bhôlô! kahiṃki sikhuchhônti?", "en": "Very good! Why are you learning it?" },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "ମୋ ସାଙ୍ଗମାନେ ଓଡ଼ିଆ। ଆଉ ମୁଁ ଓଡ଼ିଶାକୁ ଭଲ ପାଏ।", "roman": "mo saṅgômane Oṛia. au muṃ Oṛisaku bhôlô pae.", "en": "My friends are Odia. And I love Odisha." },
      { "speaker": "Bapi", "native": "ଆପଣଙ୍କୁ ଭେଟି ଖୁସି ଲାଗିଲା।", "roman": "apôṇôṅku bheṭi khusi lagila.", "en": "Nice to meet you." },
      { "speaker": "Sam", "native": "ମୋତେ ବି।", "roman": "mote bi.", "en": "Me too." }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "Odias will happily switch to English or Hindi with a foreigner — a few sentences of Odia earn real delight. People often ask personal questions early (where you're from, whether you're married, what you do); it is friendly curiosity, not rudeness. Note that many Odias say **ଆମେ** ('we') where English uses 'I', especially in the southern and western districts.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Asking and giving names",
      "tip": { "title": "Question words stay put", "body": "- Questions keep the statement order: the question word sits where the answer goes. ମୋ ନାମ **ଲିପି** → ଆପଣଙ୍କ ନାମ **କ'ଣ**?\n- 'X is Y' needs no verb: ମୋ ନାମ ସାମ୍ 'my name [is] Sam'.\n- 'Your' follows the 'you': **ଆପଣଙ୍କ** (formal), **ତୁମ / ତୁମର** (familiar). 'My' is **ମୋ**.", "pattern": "ଆପଣଙ୍କ ନାମ କ'ଣ? → ମୋ ନାମ …", "grammar": 1 },
